# Sidecar template for scripts/urdf_to_mjcf.py -- coaxial X8 + serial arm.
#
# This TEMPLATE lives in the public repository and carries NO GEOMETRY. Every
# number below is a placeholder written as `REPLACE_*` or a documented default.
# Copy it next to the private CAD export, fill it in, and keep the filled copy,
# the generated MJCF and the generated airframe out of this repository:
#
#     cp models/x8_arm.conversion.yaml.template \
#        <private-repo>/model/x8_arm.conversion.yaml
#     python scripts/urdf_to_mjcf.py <private-repo>/model/x8_arm.conversion.yaml \
#         --emit-airframe <private-repo>/model/px4/22002_mujoco_x8
#
# Rotor hub coordinates and the inertia cross-check are themselves a description
# of the airframe, which is why the filled sidecar is private too, not only the
# URDF and the STLs. The generated PX4 airframe is private for the same reason:
# its CA_ROTOR*_PX / _PY block IS the hub geometry.
#
# THE FILLED SIDECAR IS READ TWICE, and not only at conversion time:
#
#   - scripts/urdf_to_mjcf.py reads all of it, to build the MJCF and the airframe;
#   - the simulator reads the `rotors` block and `omega_idle` at every startup,
#     via --rotors / MUJOCO_SITL_ROTORS (src/mujoco_px4_sitl/rotorconfig.py).
#
# So this file is a runtime input, not a build artefact: keep it beside the MJCF
# it generated. One parser serves both readers, so an error here surfaces the same
# way in each. Editing a coefficient changes the next run with no reconversion --
# but changing one that the airframe also carries (c_t, km, omega_max, omega_idle)
# means regenerating, or PX4's allocator and the plant will disagree.
#
# What each section owns is documented in MODELING_CONVENTIONS.md section 6.
# Angles accept radians (a bare number), `{deg: 45}`, or `"45deg"`.

# --- inputs and outputs ---------------------------------------------------
# Relative paths resolve against THIS file's directory.
urdf: REPLACE_WITH_PATH_TO/kani_common_urdf_export_v3.urdf
output: REPLACE_WITH_PATH_TO/x8_arm.xml

# Decimated visual meshes are written to <output's directory>/<mesh_dir>/ and
# referenced through <compiler meshdir=...>. MuJoCo does not understand
# package:// URIs; the script rewrites them.
mesh_dir: meshes

# Set to 0.001 if the STL export is in millimetres. Confirm before trusting it:
# a 1000x model still compiles and simply never flies.
mesh_scale: 1.0

# Face budget per mesh. MuJoCo's binary STL decoder hard-refuses anything above
# 200000 faces, so a raw SolidWorks export cannot be loaded at all -- decimation
# is a requirement, not a tuning knob. Meshes are visual only.
max_faces: 60000
# Per-mesh overrides, keyed by STL stem, for parts that need more or less.
decimate:
  base_link: 30000

# Take the CAD meshes out of collision and use the primitives below instead.
# Leave true: the convex hull of 8 propellers wraps the whole vehicle, so mesh
# collision makes takeoff, landing and arm motion all hit the ground (3.2).
collision_meshes_off: true

# --- rotors ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Index IS the PX4 rotor index: HIL_ACTUATOR_CONTROLS.controls[i] drives
# rotor{i}, so this list's order must match CA_ROTOR* in the airframe file up to
# the FLU/FRD y-sign. A mismatch presents as yaw drift or attitude
# cross-coupling and is routinely misdiagnosed as an EKF fault.
#
# Order below follows PX4's own 12001_octo_cox converted to FLU
# (MODELING_CONVENTIONS.md section 4). Copy PX4's order rather than inventing
# one, so a misbehaving model can be compared against a known-good airframe.
# The coaxial pairs are (0,5) (1,4) (2,7) (3,6) -- NOT (0,4) (1,5).
#
#   spin: CCW / CW as seen from ABOVE (+1 = CCW about body +z), or +1 / -1.
#   km:   reaction torque = km * thrust, PX4's CA_ROTOR*_KM definition.
#   pos:  FLU, in base_link's frame. The z coordinate produces no torque --
#         thrust is along body +z, so r x [0,0,T] uses only x and y, and a
#         coaxial deck's height produces no torque in our model or in PX4's.
#         It does place the propeller disc (radius, below), so make it the
#         BLADE PLANE -- the mid-plane of the blade sweep -- not the motor mount.
#   radius: blade-tip radius, metres; all rotors or none. The rotor site becomes
#         a flat disc the simulator checks the arm against every frame,
#         reported and never blocked; without it that check is off and the
#         script warns. Geometry like pos, so it reaches the simulator through
#         the MJCF: change it, reconvert. A diameter here fails the overlap check.
#   c_t:  thrust coefficient, N/(rad/s)^2, i.e. T = c_t * omega^2. Fit it from the
#         motor/propeller datasheet by least squares through the origin over the
#         thrust and RPM columns (omega = rpm * 2*pi/60). Omit to fall back on
#         vehicle.py's mass-based auto-calibration, which warns on every load.
#   omega_max: MANDATORY whenever c_t is given, and from the same datasheet.
#         Only the product c_t * omega_max^2 is physical, so a measured c_t left
#         against the placeholder omega_max silently rescales every thrust in the
#         model -- it raises nothing and still "flies". The script rejects the
#         combination rather than letting it through.
#   km:   reaction torque / thrust, in metres. Fit from the torque column the same
#         way. Real props are often far from PX4's 0.05 default, and the airframe
#         file must then carry the fitted value or the allocator expects the wrong
#         yaw authority -- flyable, so it does not announce itself.
#   ct_factor: optional, (0, 1], default 1. Scales c_t for aerodynamic
#         interference -- the coaxial lower deck, in the upper rotor's wake
#         (MODELING_CONVENTIONS.md section 5). Keep c_t the isolated datasheet
#         fit and put the loss here: the plant flies c_t * ct_factor, while the
#         airframe's CA_ROTOR*_CT keeps the isolated value, so PX4 allocates the
#         decks evenly and MPC_THR_HOVER stays exact (the airframe template says
#         why). Needs a measured c_t. Changes hover and the derived gains:
#         regenerate the airframe with the MJCF.
rotors:
  - {label: front-right upper, deck: upper, spin: CCW, km: 0.05, pos: [REPLACE_X, REPLACE_-Y, REPLACE_Z_UPPER]}
  - {label: front-left  upper, deck: upper, spin: CW,  km: 0.05, pos: [REPLACE_X, REPLACE_+Y, REPLACE_Z_UPPER]}
  - {label: rear-left   upper, deck: upper, spin: CCW, km: 0.05, pos: [REPLACE_-X, REPLACE_+Y, REPLACE_Z_UPPER]}
  - {label: rear-right  upper, deck: upper, spin: CW,  km: 0.05, pos: [REPLACE_-X, REPLACE_-Y, REPLACE_Z_UPPER]}
  - {label: front-left  lower, deck: lower, spin: CCW, km: 0.05, pos: [REPLACE_X, REPLACE_+Y, REPLACE_Z_LOWER]}
  - {label: front-right lower, deck: lower, spin: CW,  km: 0.05, pos: [REPLACE_X, REPLACE_-Y, REPLACE_Z_LOWER]}
  - {label: rear-right  lower, deck: lower, spin: CCW, km: 0.05, pos: [REPLACE_-X, REPLACE_-Y, REPLACE_Z_LOWER]}
  - {label: rear-left   lower, deck: lower, spin: CW,  km: 0.05, pos: [REPLACE_-X, REPLACE_+Y, REPLACE_Z_LOWER]}
# With motor data, each entry gains c_t / omega_max / a fitted km, e.g.
#   - {label: ..., spin: CCW, km: 0.0215, c_t: 1.02e-04, omega_max: 633.4, radius: REPLACE_R, pos: [...]}
# and the lower four add a ct_factor for coaxial interference (section 5
# suggests 0.75-0.85); a datasheet is for an isolated rotor.

# Armed-but-idle rotor speed, rad/s. Shared: it is an ESC setting, not a
# per-rotor one. Omit to keep vehicle.py's placeholder (100) and its warning.
#
# This value and thrust_to_weight are the ONLY things that set MPC_THR_HOVER --
# not mass -- so changing it means rechecking the PX4 airframe file. Datasheets
# usually start around 0.4 throttle and so do not contain it; measure the armed
# idle RPM on the bench rather than extrapolating the thrust curve to zero, which
# overestimates it badly.
#
# The script prints the resulting hover command and MPC_THR_HOVER on every run.
# omega_idle: REPLACE_OMEGA_IDLE

# --- arm joints -----------------------------------------------------------
# Every arm_joint* in the URDF must appear here, or conversion fails.
#
# lower/upper are MANDATORY and load-bearing. SolidWorks writes
# `lower=upper=0` when the limits were never set in CAD, and MuJoCo compiles
# that as *UNLIMITED* -- so an unset limit is not a locked joint, it is a free
# one whose position actuator has a meaningless ctrlrange.
#
# Give the mechanism's real limits even where they let the arm into a propeller
# disc; with rotor radii set, the self-check reports how much of the envelope
# does.
#
# damping and armature are gearbox friction and reflected inertia. They are not
# cosmetic: a stiff position actuator on an undamped, inertialess hinge
# oscillates at the physics rate. armature ~ motor_rotor_inertia * ratio^2.
#
# side-channel semantics: arm_cmd.values[i] is arm_joint{i}'s ABSOLUTE angle in
# radians (section 3.4), so these signs must match the real hardware.
joints:
  arm_joint0:
    lower: REPLACE_LOWER   # e.g. {deg: -90}
    upper: REPLACE_UPPER   # e.g. {deg: 90}
    kp: 40.0               # position gain, N m / rad
    kv: 2.0                # damping on the actuator's own error
    damping: 0.5           # joint viscous damping, N m s / rad
    armature: 0.01         # reflected inertia, kg m^2
    # force_range: [-REPLACE_TORQUE, REPLACE_TORQUE]   # stall torque, N m
    # gear_ratio: REPLACE_RATIO                        # recorded, sets armature
    # home: 0.0            # stowed angle; must lie inside [lower, upper]
  arm_joint1:
    lower: REPLACE_LOWER
    upper: REPLACE_UPPER
    kp: 40.0
    kv: 2.0
    damping: 0.5
    armature: 0.01
  arm_joint2:
    lower: REPLACE_LOWER
    upper: REPLACE_UPPER
    kp: 20.0
    kv: 1.0
    damping: 0.3
    armature: 0.005
  arm_joint3:
    lower: REPLACE_LOWER
    upper: REPLACE_UPPER
    kp: 10.0
    kv: 0.5
    damping: 0.2
    armature: 0.003
  arm_joint4:
    lower: REPLACE_LOWER
    upper: REPLACE_UPPER
    kp: 5.0
    kv: 0.3
    damping: 0.1
    armature: 0.002

# --- sites ----------------------------------------------------------------
# `imu` is required: sim.py reads imu_accel / imu_gyro off it every frame. Its
# quat is forced to identity (1 0 0 0) -- every conversion in frames.py assumes
# the IMU site is aligned to the body frame.
#
# `ee` is optional (section 8). Adding it now is free; adding it later, once
# end-effector pose is in the side channel's ground_truth, costs a
# SCHEMA_VERSION bump. Put it on the last arm link, not on base_link.
sites:
  imu:
    pos: [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]        # flight controller mount, FLU, in base_link
  ee:
    body: REPLACE_LAST_ARM_LINK  # e.g. arm_link4
    pos: [REPLACE_X, REPLACE_Y, REPLACE_Z]

# --- collision primitives -------------------------------------------------
# Hand-written, replacing the CAD meshes (section 3.2). Massless by
# construction: the URDF <inertial> blocks already carry all the mass, and a
# primitive that also had density would double-count it.
#
#   box       size: [hx, hy, hz]        half-extents
#   cylinder  size: [radius, half_len]  or use fromto with size: [radius]
#   capsule   fromto: [x1,y1,z1, x2,y2,z2]  size: [radius]
#   sphere    size: [radius]
collisions:
  - name: hull
    type: box
    body: base_link
    pos: [REPLACE_X, REPLACE_Y, REPLACE_Z]
    size: [REPLACE_HX, REPLACE_HY, REPLACE_HZ]
  # One per landing-gear leg, so the vehicle rests stably and ground contact is
  # at the feet rather than at a hull corner.
  - name: leg0
    type: capsule
    body: base_link
    fromto: [REPLACE_X, REPLACE_Y, REPLACE_Z_TOP, REPLACE_X, REPLACE_Y, REPLACE_Z_FOOT]
    size: [REPLACE_RADIUS]
  # Give the arm links their own primitives too, or the arm passes through the
  # ground and through the airframe.

# --- MuJoCo options -------------------------------------------------------
# timestep is deliberately NOT accepted here: sim.py overwrites it from
# --physics-rate on every load, so setting it would be a lie (section 3.5).
option:
  integrator: RK4
  gravity: [0.0, 0.0, -9.80665]

# --- cross-check against SolidWorks --------------------------------------
# The script prints total mass, CoM and inertia. Give the CAD figures here and a
# mismatch becomes a failed check instead of something to notice later. A part
# with no material assigned is the usual cause -- and mass is what sets the
# auto-calibrated c_t, so it propagates straight into thrust.
expected_mass: REPLACE_TOTAL_MASS_KG
mass_tolerance: 0.02
expected_com: [REPLACE_X, REPLACE_Y, REPLACE_Z]

notes: >
  Fill in from the private CAD export. Frame convention is not checkable by the
  script: confirm in SolidWorks that the exported reference frame is X forward,
  Y left, Z up, with the origin at the rotor symmetry centre (section 3.1).
